      ],
      "distinction":"A provider with effective intervention powers and active supervision may have retained responsibility even when a vendor produces defective ownership records. On this reading, that defect engages substantive record-keeping requirements without itself proving that accountability was transferred. Paper allocation alone does not establish effective retention or fulfillment.",
      "family":"scope",
      "formalization":null,
      "local_id":"responsibility_allocation",
      "questions":["What evidence and applicable conduct standard distinguish effective retention from a merely contractual assertion of responsibility?"],
      "statement":"Paragraph 10 and footnote 1 principally preserve the Product Provider's ultimate accountability despite outsourced performance. Authority to obtain information, supervise and secure correction could support retention; actual supervisory conduct could support discharge under an applicable standard. Neither establishes proper records or operational soundness by itself. The packet supplies no sufficient test for concluding that the requirement has been fulfilled.",
      "subject":"Paragraph 10 as continuing allocation of responsibility"
    },
    {
      "assumptions":["Responsibility for soundness and record keeping is read substantively alongside the express obligation to ensure proper records and compatibility.","An outcome-sensitive reading does not by itself establish strict liability for every incident."],
